Unified Theoretical Framework for High-Efficiency Tandem Perovskite–Silicon Photovoltaics

Ouadi Maakoul

AI : Claude, Deepseek, chatGpt, Qwen 

Abstract

This treatise presents a self-contained mathematical framework for two-terminal tandem perovskite–silicon solar cells. Beginning with an extended detailed‑balance formalism incorporating finite external radiative efficiency, we derive a sharp upper bound for the power conversion efficiency (Theorem 1). The optical problem is treated by the Transfer Matrix Method, yielding an analytic current‑matching condition (Lemma 2.1). Charge transport is described by the Van Roosbroeck drift‑diffusion system solved via the Gummel iteration with Scharfetter–Gummel discretisation, and then extended to a fully coupled Poisson–Nernst–Planck (PNP) system that includes mobile halide vacancies. The well‑posedness of this extended PNP system is established through Schauder’s fixed‑point theorem and a novel entropy dissipation estimate (Theorem 2). From the steady‑state ion distribution we derive a dimensionally consistent, linearised formula for the ion‑migration‑induced open‑circuit voltage drift (Corollary 2.3.1). Long‑term degradation is modelled by a reaction‑diffusion equation for moisture ingress, and extreme‑value statistics yield a rigorous stochastic lower bound for the time to 80% power retention (Theorem 3). Finally, we prove that the levelised cost of electricity (LCOE) minimisation reduces to a globally solvable geometric program under posynomial surrogate models derived from the preceding theorems (Theorem 4). Every theorem is proved in detail, and all results are translated into actionable engineering design rules aligned with IEC 61215/61730 standards and industrial milestones.

---

1. Introduction and Motivation

Perovskite–silicon tandem photovoltaics have surpassed 34% certified efficiency, yet a single rigorous mathematical model that simultaneously captures efficiency limits, coupled electronic–ionic transport, and degradation kinetics is lacking. This work addresses three central challenges:

1. Efficiency limits under non‑radiative losses – how far can a real tandem cell approach the radiative Shockley–Queisser limit when the external radiative efficiency is less than unity?
2. Coupled electronic–ionic transport – what is the correct mathematical description of charge carriers and mobile ionic vacancies in a perovskite–silicon heterostructure, and does the resulting system of partial differential equations have a well‑posed solution?
3. Predictive degradation metrics – can we link microscopic ion migration and moisture ingress to macroscopic voltage loss and lifetime through analytic formulas that bypass expensive time‑dependent simulations?

We provide rigorous answers to all three questions, building a mathematical backbone that directly informs device design and manufacturing.

