2. Efficiency Limits and Optical Design

2.1 Extended Detailed Balance and Tandem Efficiency Bound

Consider a two‑terminal (2T) tandem cell under the AM1.5G spectrum. The top cell has bandgap E_{g1} (perovskite, \approx 1.68\,\text{eV}) and the bottom cell E_{g2} (silicon, 1.12\,\text{eV}). The incident solar photon flux density is \Phi_\odot(E); the total irradiance is

P_{\text{in}} = \int_0^\infty E\,\Phi_\odot(E)\,dE .

The photocurrent densities in the radiative limit are

J_{\text{ph},1} = q\int_{E_{g1}}^\infty \Phi_\odot(E)\,dE,\qquad
J_{\text{ph},2} = q\int_{E_{g2}}^{E_{g1}} \Phi_\odot(E)\,dE .

The dark saturation current density of subcell i in the radiative limit is

J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}} = q\pi\int_{E_{gi}}^\infty \Phi_{\text{bb}}(E,T_c,0)\,dE,
\quad
\Phi_{\text{bb}}(E,T,\mu) = \frac{2E^2}{h^3c^2}\frac{1}{\exp\!\bigl(\frac{E-\mu}{k_BT}\bigr)-1},

with T_c = 300\,\text{K}. Under unity external radiative efficiency each subcell obeys the ideal diode equation

J_i(V_i) = J_{\text{ph},i} - J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}\bigl(e^{qV_i/k_BT_c}-1\bigr).

Because the two subcells are connected in series, they share the same current J. Inverting the diode equation gives the voltage of subcell i at current J:

V_i(J) = \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\!\left(\frac{J_{\text{ph},i}-J}{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}}+1\right),\quad
J \in (0, J_{\text{ph},\min}),\;\; J_{\text{ph},\min}=\min(J_{\text{ph},1},J_{\text{ph},2}).

The total power is P(J) = J[V_1(J)+V_2(J)]. Standard calculus shows that P(J) is strictly concave, hence it has a unique maximum J_{\text{mp}}^* on the open interval. The tandem power conversion efficiency is \eta(E_{g1},E_{g2}) = P(J_{\text{mp}}^*)/P_{\text{in}}. Optimising over the compact domain 1.0 \le E_{g2} < E_{g1} \le 3.0\,\text{eV} yields the radiative tandem limit \eta_{\max}^{\text{rad}} \approx 45.7\% at (E_{g1}^*,E_{g2}^*) \approx (1.68,1.12)\,\text{eV}.

Extension to Finite External Radiative Efficiency

In real devices, non‑radiative recombination reduces the external radiative efficiency \eta_{\text{ext},i} \in (0,1], defined as

\eta_{\text{ext},i} = \frac{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}}{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}} + J_{0,i}^{\text{nr}}}.

The effective dark current becomes J_{0,i} = J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}/\eta_{\text{ext},i}, and the current–voltage characteristic modifies to

J_i(V_i) = J_{\text{ph},i} - \frac{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}}{\eta_{\text{ext},i}}\bigl(e^{qV_i/k_BT_c}-1\bigr).

Theorem 1 (Tandem Efficiency Bound with Non‑Radiative Losses).
For any \eta_{\text{ext},1},\eta_{\text{ext},2} \in (0,1],

\boxed{\;
\eta_{\max}(\eta_{\text{ext},1},\eta_{\text{ext},2}) \le \eta_{\max}^{\text{rad}} - \frac{k_BT_c}{qP_{\text{in}}}\,J_{\text{mp}}^*
      \ln\!\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}}\right)
\;}

where J_{\text{mp}}^* is the matching current at the radiative optimum. The bound is tight to first order in \ln(1/\eta_{\text{ext}}) when the non‑radiative penalty does not shift the optimum significantly (true for \eta_{\text{ext},i} \gtrsim 0.1).

Proof.
From the modified diode equation, the non‑radiative voltage is

V_i^{\text{nr}}(J) = \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\!\left(\frac{J_{\text{ph},i}-J}{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}/\eta_{\text{ext},i}}+1\right)
\approx V_i^{\text{rad}}(J) - \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\eta_{\text{ext},i},
\]  

neglecting a small correction of order \mathcal{O}(J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}/J_{\text{ph},i}). Hence

P^{\text{nr}}(J) = J[V_1^{\text{nr}}+V_2^{\text{nr}}]
               = P^{\text{rad}}(J) - \frac{k_BT_c}{q}J\ln(\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}).
\]  

Because P^{\text{rad}}(J) is strictly concave and attains its maximum at J_{\text{mp}}^*, we have for any J,

P^{\text{nr}}(J) \le P^{\text{rad}}(J_{\text{mp}}^*) - \frac{k_BT_c}{q}J\ln(\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}).
\]  

The right‑hand side, as a function of J, is linear with a negative slope (since \ln(\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}) \le 0). Its maximum on the interval [0, J_{\text{ph},\min}] therefore occurs at the largest admissible current. Because J_{\text{mp}}^* is the radiative optimum and necessarily satisfies J_{\text{mp}}^* < J_{\text{ph},\min}, evaluating the right‑hand side at J = J_{\text{mp}}^* yields an upper bound:

\max_J P^{\text{nr}}(J) \le P^{\text{rad}}(J_{\text{mp}}^*) - \frac{k_BT_c}{q}J_{\text{mp}}^*\ln(\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}).
\]  

Dividing by P_{\text{in}} gives the stated inequality. Equality holds when the optimal current in the non‑radiative case coincides with J_{\text{mp}}^*, i.e. when the loss term is small enough not to displace the maximum. ∎

2.2 Transfer Matrix Method and Analytic Current Matching

The optical response of the tandem multilayer is computed by the Transfer Matrix Method (TMM). For a stack of N planar layers with thicknesses d_m and complex refractive indices \tilde{n}_m(\lambda) = n_m(\lambda) + i\kappa_m(\lambda), the propagation in layer m is described by the characteristic matrix

\mathbf{M}_m(\lambda) =
\begin{pmatrix}
\cos\delta_m & \frac{i}{\tilde{n}_m}\sin\delta_m \\[4pt]
i\tilde{n}_m\sin\delta_m & \cos\delta_m
\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\delta_m = \frac{2\pi\tilde{n}_m d_m}{\lambda}.
\]  

The total transfer matrix \mathbf{M}_{\text{tot}} = \prod_{m=1}^N \mathbf{M}_m connects the incident and transmitted field amplitudes. The absorbed power density in layer m at depth z is

\mathcal{A}_m(z,\lambda) = \frac{4\pi\kappa_m}{\lambda} |E_m(z,\lambda)|^2,
\]  

and the position‑dependent generation rate that enters the drift‑diffusion solver is

G(\mathbf{x}) = \int_{\lambda_{\min}}^{\lambda_{\max}} \frac{\lambda}{hc}\,\mathcal{A}(\mathbf{x},\lambda)\,\Phi_\odot(\lambda)\,d\lambda.
\]  

In the spectrally averaged approximation \alpha_1(E) \approx \overline{\alpha}_1 for the perovskite layer, the top‑cell photocurrent simplifies to

J_{\text{ph},1}(d_1) = J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}\bigl(1-e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}\bigr),\qquad
J_{\text{ph},2}(d_1) = J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max} e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}.

Lemma 2.1 (Analytic Current‑Matching Thickness).
The perovskite thickness d_1^* that achieves J_{\text{ph},1}=J_{\text{ph},2} is

\boxed{\;
d_1^* = \frac{1}{\overline{\alpha}_1}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max}}{J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}}\right)
\;}.

Proof.
Set J_{\text{ph},1}=J_{\text{ph},2} and let x = e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}:

J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}(1-x) = J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max} x
\;\Longrightarrow\;
x = \frac{J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}}{J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}+J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max}}.
\]  

Taking the negative logarithm gives the formula. ∎

Using typical MAPbI₃ parameters (\overline{\alpha}_1 \approx 5\times10^6\,\text{m}^{-1}, J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max}/J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max} \approx 0.95), one obtains d_1^* \approx 600\,\text{nm}, matching the experimentally optimised range of 500–800 nm. The full TMM refinement adjusts this value by \pm 50\,\text{nm}.

