3. Electronic Charge Transport and Numerical Solution

3.1 The Van Roosbroeck Drift‑Diffusion System

In the absence of mobile ions, the electronic transport in each subcell is described by the Van Roosbroeck system:

-\nabla\cdot(\epsilon\nabla\phi) = q\,(p - n + N_D^+ - N_A^-),

\frac{\partial n}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{q}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_n + G - R(n,p),\qquad
\frac{\partial p}{\partial t} = -\frac{1}{q}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_p + G - R(n,p),

\mathbf{J}_n = q\mu_n n(-\nabla\phi) + qD_n\nabla n,\quad
\mathbf{J}_p = q\mu_p p(-\nabla\phi) - qD_p\nabla p,

with the Einstein relations D_{n,p} = \mu_{n,p} V_T where V_T = k_BT/q. The net recombination rate combines radiative, Shockley–Read–Hall, and Auger processes:

R(n,p) = B(np-n_i^2) + \frac{np-n_i^2}{\tau_p(n+n_1)+\tau_n(p+p_1)} + (C_n n + C_p p)(np-n_i^2).

3.2 Gummel Iteration and Scharfetter–Gummel Discretisation

The system is solved numerically by the Gummel iteration, which decouples the equations:

1. Solve the nonlinear Poisson equation for \phi with fixed carrier densities.
2. Solve the electron continuity equation for n with frozen \phi and p.
3. Solve the hole continuity equation for p with frozen \phi and n.

For the drift‑dominated fluxes we use the Scharfetter–Gummel discretisation, which ensures positivity and exponential accuracy. On a 1D mesh with node spacing h_j, the electron current between nodes j and j+1 is

J_{n,j+1/2} = q\frac{D_{n,j+1/2}}{h_j}
\left[ B\!\left(-\frac{q\Delta\phi_{j+1/2}}{k_BT}\right) n_{j+1}
      - B\!\left(\frac{q\Delta\phi_{j+1/2}}{k_BT}\right) n_j \right],

where \Delta\phi_{j+1/2} = \phi_{j+1}-\phi_j and B(x)=x/(e^x-1) is the Bernoulli function. The scheme is unconditionally stable when combined with an implicit time integrator and converges with \mathcal{O}(h^2) spatial accuracy.

