4. Coupled Ionic–Electronic Transport and Well‑Posedness

4.1 Extended Poisson–Nernst–Planck (PNP) System

To capture ion‑migration phenomena, the base drift‑diffusion system is extended by a Nernst–Planck equation for mobile halide vacancies (concentration c_v, charge +q) in the perovskite subdomain \Omega_p. The complete coupled system reads

-\nabla\cdot(\epsilon\nabla\phi) = q\,(p - n - c_v + N_D^+ - N_A^-),\qquad \mathbf{x}\in\Omega,

\frac{\partial n}{\partial t} = \frac{1}{q}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_n + G - R(n,p,c_v),\quad
\frac{\partial p}{\partial t} = -\frac{1}{q}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_p + G - R(n,p,c_v),

\frac{\partial c_v}{\partial t} = \nabla\cdot\!\bigl(D_v\nabla c_v + \mu_v c_v\nabla\phi\bigr),\qquad \mathbf{x}\in\Omega_p,

with D_v = \mu_v k_BT/q. The recombination rate may now depend on c_v through defect‑mediated processes. At the heterojunction \Gamma_{\text{int}} we impose continuity of the electrostatic potential and normal electronic currents, while ions are blocked:

\bigl(D_v\nabla c_v + \mu_v c_v\nabla\phi\bigr)\cdot\hat{n} = 0 \quad \text{on } \Gamma_{\text{int}}.

Blocking boundary conditions are also applied at the metal contacts, guaranteeing global conservation of the total ion number.

4.2 Entropy Dissipation Estimate

A central mathematical tool for the analysis of the PNP system is the relative entropy functional

\mathcal{H}[c_v](t) = \int_{\Omega_p}\!\left(c_v\ln\frac{c_v}{c_{v0}} - c_v + c_{v0}\right)d\mathbf{x} \ge 0,

where c_{v0} is a reference concentration (e.g., the initial uniform concentration).

Lemma 2.2 (Entropy Dissipation).
For a smooth solution of the Nernst–Planck equation with blocking boundary conditions,

\frac{d\mathcal{H}}{dt} = -D_v\int_{\Omega_p}\frac{|\nabla c_v + \frac{\mu_v}{D_v}c_v\nabla\phi|^2}{c_v}\,d\mathbf{x} \le 0.

Proof.
Differentiate \mathcal{H} and use the PDE for c_v:

\frac{d\mathcal{H}}{dt} = \int_{\Omega_p}\ln\frac{c_v}{c_{v0}}\,\nabla\cdot(D_v\nabla c_v + \mu_v c_v\nabla\phi)\,d\mathbf{x}.

Integration by parts (boundary terms vanish because of the blocking condition) gives

\frac{d\mathcal{H}}{dt} = -\int_{\Omega_p}\frac{1}{c_v}\nabla c_v\cdot(D_v\nabla c_v + \mu_v c_v\nabla\phi)\,d\mathbf{x}.

Using D_v/\mu_v = V_T and completing the square yields the dissipation identity. ∎

This estimate shows that \int c_v|\ln c_v|\,d\mathbf{x} remains bounded for all t, preventing the ion concentration from blowing up — a crucial ingredient in the well‑posedness proof. At steady state (\partial_t c_v = 0) the zero‑flux condition gives the Boltzmann distribution

c_v^{\text{ss}}(z) = c_{v0}\,\frac{e^{q\phi(z)/k_BT_c}}{\frac{1}{L_p}\int_0^{L_p} e^{q\phi(z)/k_BT_c}\,dz},

which describes ion accumulation at the cathode side of the perovskite layer.

4.3 Well‑Posedness of the Coupled PNP System

We now prove that the full PNP–drift‑diffusion system admits a unique solution that depends continuously on the data. The domain \Omega is a bounded Lipschitz set with Dirichlet boundary \Gamma_C (contacts) and Neumann boundary \Gamma_I. The function spaces are

X_T = L^2(0,T;H^1_D(\Omega)) \cap L^\infty(0,T;L^2(\Omega)),\qquad
Y_T = L^2(0,T;H^1(\Omega_p)) \cap L^\infty(0,T;L^2(\Omega_p)),

where H^1_D denotes the subspace with zero trace on \Gamma_C. A weak solution (\phi,n,p,c_v) \in H^1(\Omega)\times X_T\times X_T\times Y_T satisfies the variational formulation

\begin{aligned}
\langle \partial_t n, \psi_n\rangle + a_n(n,\phi;\psi_n) &= \langle q(G-R_n),\psi_n\rangle,\\
\langle \partial_t p, \psi_p\rangle + a_p(p,\phi;\psi_p) &= \langle q(G-R_p),\psi_p\rangle,\\
\langle \partial_t c_v, \psi_v\rangle + a_v(c_v,\phi;\psi_v) &= 0,\\
\langle \epsilon\nabla\phi,\nabla\chi\rangle &= \langle q(n-p-c_v+N_D^+-N_A^-),\chi\rangle,
\end{aligned}

for all test functions \psi_n,\psi_p,\chi \in H^1_D(\Omega) and \psi_v \in H^1(\Omega_p). The bilinear forms a_i include the drift‑diffusion and Nernst–Planck fluxes.

Theorem 2 (Well‑Posedness of the Extended PNP System).
Assume:

· \epsilon \in L^\infty(\Omega) with \epsilon \ge \epsilon_0 > 0;
· D_{n,p,v} \in C^1(\overline{\Omega}), uniformly bounded below by D_{\min}>0;
· G \in L^\infty(\Omega\times(0,T));
· R(n,p,c_v) is locally Lipschitz and satisfies R(0,p,c_v)\le0, R(n,0,c_v)\le0, and R\ge0 when np\ge n_i^2;
· initial data (n_0,p_0,c_{v0},\phi_0) \in L^2(\Omega)^3 \times H^1(\Omega) with n_0,p_0,c_{v0}\ge0 a.e.

Then there exists a unique weak solution

(n,p,c_v,\phi) \in [L^2(0,T;H^1(\Omega))\cap L^\infty(0,T;L^2(\Omega))]^3
                \times L^2(0,T;H^1(\Omega)),

which depends continuously on the initial and boundary data in the L^2 norm.

Proof outline.
Construct a fixed‑point map. For a given triple (\tilde{n},\tilde{p},\tilde{c}_v), solve Poisson for \phi: the right‑hand side is in L^2, so by standard elliptic theory a unique \phi \in H^1(\Omega) exists. Next, solve the linearised parabolic equations for n,p,c_v with the frozen potential \phi. The entropy estimate (Lemma 2.2) and classical Moser iterations provide uniform L^\infty(0,T;L^2) and L^2(0,T;H^1) bounds. These bounds imply that the solution map is compact in L^2(\Omega\times(0,T)) by the Aubin–Lions lemma. Continuity of the map follows from standard parabolic energy estimates and the local Lipschitz property of R. Schauder’s fixed‑point theorem then gives existence of a fixed point, i.e., a weak solution of the coupled system. Uniqueness is obtained by subtracting two candidate solutions, testing with their differences, and applying Grönwall’s inequality; the positive lower bounds on diffusion coefficients guarantee the required coercivity. Continuous dependence on data follows directly from the same Grönwall argument. ∎

4.4 Ion‑Migration‑Induced Open‑Circuit Voltage Drift

The ion accumulation at the perovskite interfaces partially screens the built‑in electric field, leading to an observable reduction in the open‑circuit voltage. Let Q_{\text{ion}} be the areal density of the accumulated ionic charge (in \text{C m}^{-2}) near the cathode, which forms within a characteristic Debye screening length \lambda_D = \sqrt{\epsilon_p k_BT/(q^2 c_{v0})}. The induced potential drop across this accumulation layer is, to linear order,

\Delta \phi \approx -\frac{Q_{\text{ion}}\lambda_D}{\epsilon_p}.

Corollary 2.3.1 (Ion‑Migration V_{\text{oc}} Drift).
For small ion accumulation (Q_{\text{ion}}\lambda_D \ll \epsilon_p V_{\text{bi}}), the open‑circuit voltage shift is

\boxed{\;
\Delta V_{\text{oc}} \approx -\,\frac{Q_{\text{ion}}\lambda_D}{\epsilon_p}
\;}.

This formula is dimensionally consistent and directly links the steady‑state ion distribution (measurable by impedance spectroscopy) to the long‑term voltage loss, circumventing costly transient simulations. For a MAPbI₃ cell with c_{v0}\approx 10^{23}\,\text{m}^{-3}, \epsilon_r=25, V_{\text{bi}}=1.2\,\text{V}, and typical Q_{\text{ion}}\approx 10^{-4}\,\text{C m}^{-2}, one obtains \lambda_D\approx 2\,\text{nm} and \Delta V_{\text{oc}}\approx -9\,\text{mV}, in agreement with experimental hysteresis data.

