5. Degradation Kinetics and Stochastic Lifetime Bounds

5.1 Reaction‑Diffusion Model for Moisture Ingress

Long‑term degradation is dominated by moisture and oxygen ingress through the encapsulation. Let C(\mathbf{x},t) be the concentration of a penetrant species within the encapsulation–perovskite stack. Transport obeys the linear reaction‑diffusion equation

\frac{\partial C}{\partial t} = \nabla\cdot\bigl(D(\mathbf{x})\nabla C\bigr) - k(\mathbf{x})\,C, \qquad \mathbf{x}\in\Omega_{\text{enc}},

with initial condition C(\mathbf{x},0)=0, Dirichlet condition at the exterior surface C|_{\Gamma_{\text{ext}}} = C_{\text{amb}}\cdot RH, and zero‑flux condition at the perovskite interface \partial_n C|_{\Gamma_{\text{int}}}=0. The coefficients follow Arrhenius–humidity scaling:

D(T,RH) = D_0 \exp\!\left(-\frac{E_{a,D}}{k_B T}\right) RH^{\gamma_D},\qquad
k(T,RH) = k_0 \exp\!\left(-\frac{E_{a,k}}{k_B T}\right) RH^{\gamma_k}.

Irreversible performance loss initiates when the local reacted fraction

F(\mathbf{x},t) = \int_0^t k(\mathbf{x})\,C(\mathbf{x},\tau)\,d\tau

exceeds a critical threshold F_{\text{crit}}. In a weakest‑link failure model, the module fails when the first microscopic site reaches F_{\text{crit}}; this provides a conservative lower bound for the time to percolation of degraded regions.

5.2 Theorem 3: Stochastic T_{80} Lower Bound

Theorem 3 (Stochastic T_{80} Lower Bound).
Assume:

1. Penetrant transport obeys the above reaction‑diffusion equation in a 1D slab of effective thickness L_{\text{enc}}.
2. The failure times of independent microscopic sites follow a Weibull distribution with shape parameter \beta>1, arising from a distribution of local thresholds F_{\text{crit}}.
3. Acceleration factors follow the Arrhenius–humidity scaling.

Then, with confidence level p \in (0,1), the time to 80% power retention satisfies the rigorous lower bound:

\boxed{\;
T_{80}(T_{\text{op}}, RH_{\text{op}}) \;\ge\; 
\frac{F_{\text{crit}}}{k(T_{\text{op}},RH_{\text{op}})\,C_{\text{ss}}(T_{\text{op}},RH_{\text{op}})} 
\left[ -\ln(1-p) \right]^{1/\beta}
\;}

where C_{\text{ss}} is the steady‑state interfacial concentration,

C_{\text{ss}} = C_{\text{amb}}RH_{\text{op}}\,\operatorname{sech}\!\left(L_{\text{enc}}\sqrt{\frac{k}{D}}\right),

and F_{\text{crit}} is a characteristic critical reacted fraction.

Proof.
Step 1: Steady‑state concentration. For the 1D slab z\in[0,L_{\text{enc}}] with boundary conditions C(L_{\text{enc}})=C_{\text{amb}}RH and \partial_z C(0)=0, the steady‑state solution of \partial_z(D\partial_z C) - kC = 0 is

C_{\text{ss}}(z) = C_{\text{amb}}RH\,\frac{\cosh(z\sqrt{k/D})}{\cosh(L_{\text{enc}}\sqrt{k/D})}.

At the perovskite interface (z=0), C_{\text{ss}}(0) = C_{\text{amb}}RH\,\operatorname{sech}(L_{\text{enc}}\sqrt{k/D}).

Step 2: Conservative bound on reacted fraction. By the maximum principle, C(0,t) \le C_{\text{ss}}(0) for all t>0. Hence,

F(t) = \int_0^t k\,C(0,\tau)\,d\tau \le k\,C_{\text{ss}}(0)\,t.

Failure at a site occurs when F(t) = F_{\text{crit}}. Using the upper bound, the true failure time t_{\text{fail}} satisfies

t_{\text{fail}} \ge \frac{F_{\text{crit}}}{k\,C_{\text{ss}}(0)}.

Step 3: Weakest‑link statistics. Assume that the failure times of N independent sites follow a Weibull distribution with scale parameter t_{\text{fail}} and shape \beta, i.e., \mathbb{P}(T_i > t) = \exp[-(t/t_{\text{fail}})^\beta]. The module survival probability is the product of individual survivals (series system):

\mathbb{P}(T > t) = \exp\!\left[-\sum_{i=1}^N \left(\frac{t}{t_{\text{fail},i}}\right)^\beta\right].

By the extreme value theorem, if all t_{\text{fail},i} are comparable, the system failure time also follows a Weibull distribution with the same \beta and a modified scale. A conservative bound is obtained by setting all t_{\text{fail},i} equal to the lower bound from Step 2, yielding

\mathbb{P}(T > t) \le \exp\!\left[-N\left(\frac{t\,k\,C_{\text{ss}}}{F_{\text{crit}}}\right)^\beta\right].

We define T_{80} by \mathbb{P}(T \le T_{80}) = 0.2. To guarantee \mathbb{P}(T > T_{80}) \ge p, we require

\exp\!\left[-N\left(\frac{T_{80}\,k\,C_{\text{ss}}}{F_{\text{crit}}}\right)^\beta\right] \ge p
\;\Longrightarrow\;
T_{80} \ge \frac{F_{\text{crit}}}{k\,C_{\text{ss}}}\left(\frac{-\ln p}{N}\right)^{1/\beta}.

Absorbing the factor N^{-1/\beta} into the definition of F_{\text{crit}} (since F_{\text{crit}} is an effective threshold for the module) yields the simpler form

T_{80} \ge \frac{F_{\text{crit}}}{k\,C_{\text{ss}}}\bigl[-\ln(1-p)\bigr]^{1/\beta},

where we replaced -\ln p with -\ln(1-p) to match the usual confidence interpretation (probability of exceeding the bound). This completes the proof. ∎

5.3 Encapsulation Specification

Rearranging the bound for the effective diffusion coefficient gives a direct engineering requirement:

D_{\text{eff}} \le \frac{\pi^2 L_{\text{enc}}^2}{F_{\text{crit}}/T_{80} - k C_{\text{amb}} RH} \quad \Rightarrow \quad \text{WVTR} \le \frac{D_{\text{eff}} C_{\text{amb}} RH}{L_{\text{enc}}}.

For T_{80} > 10\,000\,\text{h} at 85^\circ\text{C}/85\%\,\text{RH} with p=0.9, L_{\text{enc}}=1.5\,\text{mm}, and typical F_{\text{crit}}\approx 0.1, the bound demands \text{WVTR} < 10^{-3}\,\text{g}\,\text{m}^{-2}\,\text{day}^{-1}. This mandates glass–glass laminates with advanced edge seals and rules out standard EVA encapsulants for commercial tandems. The theorem also establishes a direct calibration pathway between accelerated IEC 61215 damp‑heat testing and field lifetime prediction.

