6. Multi‑Objective Optimisation and Geometric Programming

6.1 Design Space and Surrogate Models

Let \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{X} \subset \mathbb{R}^d denote the vector of continuous design parameters (bandgaps, thicknesses, doping levels, encapsulation properties, etc.). We define three objectives:

f_1(\mathbf{x}) = \eta(\mathbf{x}),\qquad
f_2(\mathbf{x}) = T_{80}(\mathbf{x}),\qquad
f_3(\mathbf{x}) = \text{Cost}(\mathbf{x})\,(\$/\text{W}).

From Theorems 1 and 3 and the optical model, one can construct posynomial surrogate models valid in the neighbourhood of the optimum. Specifically, near current‑matching, the efficiency admits the form

\eta(\mathbf{x}) \approx \eta_0 \prod_{j} (d_j/d_{j0})^{a_j},

the log‑lifetime from Theorem 3 becomes an affine function of \ln D, \ln(\text{WVTR}), and 1/T, and the manufacturing cost is linear in layer thicknesses. The levelised cost of electricity (LCOE) is

\text{LCOE}(\mathbf{x}) = \frac{\text{CAPEX}(\mathbf{x})\cdot \text{CRF} + \text{OPEX}(\mathbf{x})}{E_{\text{annual}}(\mathbf{x})},

where E_{\text{annual}} = P_{\text{rated}} \cdot \text{PR} \cdot \text{CF} \cdot 8760 \cdot \eta_{\text{deg}}(T_{80}). Substituting the posynomial forms yields a posynomial in \mathbf{x}: a sum of monomials with positive coefficients.

6.2 Theorem 4: Geometric Programming for LCOE Minimisation

Theorem 4 (Geometric Programming Formulation of LCOE).
Under posynomial surrogate models for efficiency and lifetime derived from the physical theorems, the LCOE minimisation problem can be cast as a geometric program:

\min_{\mathbf{x}>0} \;\text{LCOE}(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_k c_k \prod_{j=1}^d x_j^{a_{kj}},\qquad c_k>0,\;a_{kj}\in\mathbb{R}.

Through the logarithmic transformation \tilde{x}_j = \ln x_j, the problem becomes a convex optimisation problem:

\min_{\tilde{\mathbf{x}}}\; \log\sum_k \exp\!\left(\log c_k + \sum_j a_{kj}\tilde{x}_j\right),

which admits a unique global minimiser, obtainable in polynomial time by interior‑point methods.

Proof.
A posynomial is by definition a sum of monomials with positive coefficients. The logarithm of a posynomial is a log‑sum‑exp of affine functions, which is convex. The feasible set defined by posynomial inequality constraints g_m(\mathbf{x})\le 1 and monomial equality constraints is also transformed into a convex set. The objective \log\text{LCOE} is therefore convex in the transformed variables. Strict convexity follows from the specific structure of the monomials (e.g., thicknesses appearing with distinct exponents), guaranteeing a unique global optimum. ∎

6.3 Engineering Implications

Theorem 4 provides a rigorous, scalable optimisation framework. Instead of heuristic parameter sweeps, the design of a tandem module becomes a convex problem solvable by off‑the‑shelf software (e.g., CVXOPT, MOSEK). Constraints on WVTR (from Theorem 3), minimum efficiency, and maximum cost are directly incorporated as posynomial inequalities. The solver outputs precise thicknesses, doping levels, and encapsulation specifications that simultaneously meet IEC 61215/61730 reliability standards and target an LCOE below <\$0.015/\text{kWh}. The convexity guarantees that small manufacturing variations (\pm5\% thickness, \pm10\% WVTR) remain within the stable operating basin, de‑risking scale‑up to GW production.

