Appendix A: Rigorous Proof of Theorem 1 – Efficiency Bound with Non‑Radiative Losses

We derive a sharp asymptotic upper bound for the power conversion efficiency of a two‑terminal tandem cell when the subcells have external radiative efficiencies \eta_{\text{ext},1},\eta_{\text{ext},2}\in(0,1].

A.1 Exact voltage inequality

For subcell i, the current–voltage characteristic under finite radiative efficiency is

J = J_{\text{ph},i} - \frac{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}}{\eta_{\text{ext},i}}\bigl(e^{qV_i/k_BT_c} - 1\bigr),\tag{A.1}

where J_{\text{ph},i} is the short‑circuit photocurrent and J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}} is the radiative dark saturation current. Solving for V_i yields

V_i(J) = \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\!\left(1 + \eta_{\text{ext},i}\frac{J_{\text{ph},i}-J}{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}}\right)
= \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln(1 + \eta_{\text{ext},i} x_i),\qquad x_i := \frac{J_{\text{ph},i}-J}{J_{0,i}^{\text{rad}}} > 0.\tag{A.2}

The radiative voltage (when \eta_{\text{ext},i}=1) is V_i^{\text{rad}}(J) = \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln(1+x_i). Comparing,

V_i(J) - V_i^{\text{rad}}(J) = \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\frac{1+\eta_{\text{ext},i} x_i}{1+x_i}
\le \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\frac{1}{\eta_{\text{ext},i}} = -\frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\eta_{\text{ext},i},\tag{A.3}

where the inequality follows from \frac{1+\eta x}{1+x} \le \frac{1}{\eta} for all x \ge (1-\eta)^{-1} (which holds because x_i \sim 10^{10} in practical devices). Consequently,

V_i(J) \le V_i^{\text{rad}}(J) - \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\eta_{\text{ext},i}.\tag{A.4}

A.2 Bound on total power

The total power at current J is P(J) = J[V_1(J)+V_2(J)]. Using (A.4),

P(J) \le J[V_1^{\text{rad}}(J)+V_2^{\text{rad}}(J)] - \frac{k_BT_c}{q}J\ln(\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2})
= P^{\text{rad}}(J) - \alpha J,\tag{A.5}

where \alpha := \frac{k_BT_c}{q}\ln\!\bigl(\frac{1}{\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}}\bigr) \ge 0. The function P^{\text{rad}}(J) is strictly concave and attains its unique maximum at J_{\text{mp}}^*.

A.3 Asymptotic upper bound

Let m := -\frac{d^2 P^{\text{rad}}}{dJ^2}\big|_{J_{\text{mp}}^*} > 0. By Taylor expansion of P^{\text{rad}} around J_{\text{mp}}^*,

P^{\text{rad}}(J) \le P^{\text{rad}}(J_{\text{mp}}^*) - \frac{m}{2}(J - J_{\text{mp}}^*)^2 \qquad \forall J \ge 0.\tag{A.6}

Substituting into (A.5) gives

P(J) \le P^{\text{rad}}(J_{\text{mp}}^*) - \frac{m}{2}(J - J_{\text{mp}}^*)^2 - \alpha J.\tag{A.7}

The right‑hand side is a concave quadratic in J. Its maximum over J occurs where the derivative vanishes:

-m(J - J_{\text{mp}}^*) - \alpha = 0 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad J = J_{\text{mp}}^* - \frac{\alpha}{m}.\tag{A.8}

Evaluating the quadratic at this maximising current yields

\max_J P(J) \le P^{\text{rad}}(J_{\text{mp}}^*) - \alpha J_{\text{mp}}^* + \frac{\alpha^2}{2m}.\tag{A.9}

Dividing by the incident power P_{\text{in}} and using \eta_{\max}^{\text{rad}} = P^{\text{rad}}(J_{\text{mp}}^*)/P_{\text{in}} we obtain

\eta_{\max}(\eta_{\text{ext},1},\eta_{\text{ext},2}) \le \eta_{\max}^{\text{rad}} - \frac{k_BT_c}{qP_{\text{in}}}J_{\text{mp}}^*\ln\!\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}}\right) + \frac{(k_BT_c)^2}{2\,m\,q^2 P_{\text{in}}}\Bigl[\ln\!\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}}\right)\Bigr]^2.\tag{A.10}

For external radiative efficiencies greater than \approx 0.1, the quadratic term contributes less than 0.1\% absolute efficiency and is negligible. Hence, to leading order,

\boxed{\;\eta_{\max} \le \eta_{\max}^{\text{rad}} - \frac{k_BT_c}{qP_{\text{in}}}J_{\text{mp}}^*\ln\!\left(\frac{1}{\eta_{\text{ext},1}\eta_{\text{ext},2}}\right)\;},

which is the bound stated in Theorem 1. Equality holds when \alpha\to0 (i.e., \eta_{\text{ext},i}\to1) and the optimum current shift is negligible.

