Appendix B: Transfer Matrix Method and Derivation of Lemma 2.1

Consider a planar multilayer stack of N layers with thicknesses d_m and complex refractive indices \tilde{n}_m = n_m + i\kappa_m. For a normally incident plane wave of wavelength \lambda, the electric field in layer m satisfies the Helmholtz equation. The tangential field components at the interface between layer m and m+1 are connected by the characteristic matrix

\mathbf{M}_m = \begin{pmatrix}
\cos\delta_m & \frac{i}{\tilde{n}_m}\sin\delta_m \\
i\tilde{n}_m\sin\delta_m & \cos\delta_m
\end{pmatrix},\quad \delta_m = \frac{2\pi\tilde{n}_m d_m}{\lambda}.\tag{B.1}

The total transfer matrix is \mathbf{M}_{\text{tot}} = \prod_{m=1}^N \mathbf{M}_m. The complex reflection and transmission coefficients follow from the elements of \mathbf{M}_{\text{tot}}. The time‑averaged absorbed power density in layer m at depth z is

\mathcal{A}_m(z,\lambda) = \frac{4\pi\kappa_m}{\lambda}\,|E_m(z,\lambda)|^2.\tag{B.2}

The generation rate of electron–hole pairs is obtained by integrating over the solar spectrum:

G(z) = \int_{\lambda_{\min}}^{\lambda_{\max}} \frac{\lambda}{hc}\,\mathcal{A}_m(z,\lambda)\,\Phi_\odot(\lambda)\,d\lambda.\tag{B.3}

Spectrally averaged approximation. When the perovskite absorption coefficient \alpha(\lambda) = 4\pi\kappa(\lambda)/\lambda varies slowly over the relevant spectral range, we replace it by a flux‑weighted average \overline{\alpha}_1. The top‑cell photocurrent then becomes

J_{\text{ph},1}(d_1) = q\int_{E_{g1}}^\infty \Phi_\odot(E)\bigl(1-e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}\bigr)\,dE
= J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}\bigl(1-e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}\bigr),\tag{B.4}

where J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max} = q\int_{E_{g1}}^\infty \Phi_\odot(E)\,dE. The bottom‑cell current is the transmitted fraction,

J_{\text{ph},2}(d_1) = J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max} e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1},\qquad
J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max} = q\int_{E_{g2}}^{E_{g1}} \Phi_\odot(E)\,dE.\tag{B.5}

Proof of Lemma 2.1. Setting J_{\text{ph},1}=J_{\text{ph},2} gives

J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}(1-e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}) = J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max} e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}.

Let x = e^{-\overline{\alpha}_1 d_1}. Then

J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}(1-x) = J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max} x
\;\Longrightarrow\;
x = \frac{J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}}{J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}+J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max}}.

Hence d_1^* = -\frac{1}{\overline{\alpha}_1}\ln x = \frac{1}{\overline{\alpha}_1}\ln\!\left(1+\frac{J_{\text{ph},2}^{\max}}{J_{\text{ph},1}^{\max}}\right). ∎

