Appendix C: Scharfetter–Gummel Discretisation of the Drift‑Diffusion Equations

We derive the exponentially fitted scheme for the electron current density J_n = q\mu_n n(-\nabla\phi) + qD_n\nabla n in one dimension. On a segment [z_j, z_{j+1}] with length h_j and constant electric field E = -\Delta\phi/h_j, the electron continuity equation reduces to

J_n = q\mu_n n E + qD_n \frac{dn}{dz} = \text{const}.

Using the Einstein relation D_n = \mu_n V_T with V_T = k_BT/q, rewrite as

\frac{dn}{dz} + \frac{E}{V_T} n = \frac{J_n}{qD_n}.

The solution with boundary values n(z_j)=n_j and n(z_{j+1})=n_{j+1} is

n(z) = \left(n_j + \frac{J_n}{q\mu_n E}\right)e^{-(z-z_j)E/V_T} - \frac{J_n}{q\mu_n E}.

Evaluating at z_{j+1} and solving for J_n yields

J_{n,j+1/2} = \frac{qD_n}{h_j}\bigl[\,B(-\Delta\phi/V_T)\,n_{j+1} - B(\Delta\phi/V_T)\,n_j\,\bigr],\tag{C.1}

where B(x) = x/(e^x-1) is the Bernoulli function. This expression reduces to the central difference scheme in the diffusion‑dominated limit (\Delta\phi\to0) and to the upwind flux in the drift‑dominated limit (|\Delta\phi|\gg V_T). The hole current is discretised analogously with appropriate sign changes. The Gummel iteration then solves the nonlinear Poisson equation (by Newton’s method) and the linearised continuity equations successively, using the Scharfetter–Gummel fluxes for the current components.

