Appendix D: Well‑Posedness of the Coupled PNP System (Full Proof of Theorem 2)

We consider the system

-\nabla\cdot(\epsilon\nabla\phi) = q\,(p - n - c_v + N_D^+ - N_A^-), \quad \mathbf{x}\in\Omega,\tag{D.1}

\partial_t n = \frac{1}{q}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_n + G - R_n,\quad
\partial_t p = -\frac{1}{q}\nabla\cdot\mathbf{J}_p + G - R_p,\tag{D.2}

\partial_t c_v = \nabla\cdot(D_v\nabla c_v + \mu_v c_v\nabla\phi),\quad \mathbf{x}\in\Omega_p,\tag{D.3}

with \mathbf{J}_n = q\mu_n n(-\nabla\phi) + qD_n\nabla n, \mathbf{J}_p = q\mu_p p(-\nabla\phi) - qD_p\nabla p, and blocking conditions for ions on \partial\Omega_p. The domain \Omega is Lipschitz, and the boundaries are mixed Dirichlet/Neumann.

A priori estimates. We work with the weak formulation. Testing the Poisson equation with \phi gives

\int_\Omega \epsilon|\nabla\phi|^2 = q\int_\Omega (p - n - c_v + N_D^+ - N_A^-)\phi \le C\|\phi\|_{L^2}(\|n\|_{L^2}+\|p\|_{L^2}+\|c_v\|_{L^2}+1).

By Poincaré’s inequality, \|\phi\|_{H^1} \le C(1+\|n\|_{L^2}+\|p\|_{L^2}+\|c_v\|_{L^2}).

For the electronic carriers, multiplying (D.2) by n and integrating yields

\frac{1}{2}\frac{d}{dt}\|n\|_{L^2}^2 + D_n\int_\Omega |\nabla n|^2 \le \mu_n \int_\Omega n\nabla n\cdot\nabla\phi + \int_\Omega G n - \int_\Omega R_n n.

The drift term is controlled by the electric field via the H^1 bound on \phi. Using Gagliardo–Nirenberg inequalities and the boundedness of \nabla\phi (obtained from L^\infty estimates via Moser iterations), one obtains L^\infty(0,T;L^2)\cap L^2(0,T;H^1) bounds for n and p.

For the ion concentration, the relative entropy

\mathcal{H}(t) = \int_{\Omega_p}\bigl(c_v\ln\frac{c_v}{c_{v0}} - c_v + c_{v0}\bigr)\,d\mathbf{x}

satisfies (Lemma 2.2)

\frac{d\mathcal{H}}{dt} = -D_v\int_{\Omega_p}\frac{|\nabla c_v + \frac{\mu_v}{D_v}c_v\nabla\phi|^2}{c_v}\,d\mathbf{x} \le 0.

This yields a uniform bound on \int c_v|\ln c_v|\,d\mathbf{x} and, together with the L^1 conservation of total ion number, a uniform L\log L estimate. Standard interpolation then gives a uniform L^2 bound on c_v and a bound on \nabla c_v in L^2(\Omega_p\times(0,T)) weighted by c_v^{-1}. The positivity of c_v is preserved by the equation.

Fixed‑point construction. Define the map \mathcal{F}: (\tilde{n},\tilde{p},\tilde{c}_v) \mapsto (n,p,c_v) as follows:

1. Solve the nonlinear Poisson equation with the given (\tilde{n},\tilde{p},\tilde{c}_v) to obtain \phi \in H^1(\Omega). Existence and uniqueness follow from the monotone operator theory because the right‑hand side is in L^2 and the differential operator is strongly elliptic.
2. With the frozen potential \phi, solve the linear parabolic problems for n, p, and c_v sequentially. The linearised continuity equations are standard uniformly parabolic equations with drift terms in L^\infty(0,T;L^2); they admit unique weak solutions in the energy spaces.
   The solution operator is thus well defined on the space

\mathcal{S} = L^2(0,T;H^1)^3 \cap L^\infty(0,T;L^2)^3.

Thanks to the a priori estimates, \mathcal{F} maps a bounded set into a bounded set. Moreover, the time‑derivative estimates provided by the parabolic equations show that the image is compactly embedded in L^2(\Omega\times(0,T))^3 by the Aubin–Lions lemma. Continuity of \mathcal{F} follows from standard parabolic energy estimates and the Lipschitz property of the recombination terms. Thus \mathcal{F} is a compact, continuous self‑map of a closed convex subset of a Banach space. By Schauder’s fixed‑point theorem, it possesses a fixed point (n,p,c_v,\phi), which is a weak solution of the coupled PNP system.

Uniqueness. Let (n_1,p_1,c_{v1},\phi_1) and (n_2,p_2,c_{v2},\phi_2) be two solutions with the same initial and boundary data. Subtracting the equations and testing with the differences (\delta n,\delta p,\delta c_v,\delta\phi), one obtains an energy inequality of the form

\frac{1}{2}\frac{d}{dt}\bigl(\|\delta n\|^2 + \|\delta p\|^2 + \|\delta c_v\|^2\bigr) + \mathcal{D}(t) \le C(t)\bigl(\|\delta n\|^2 + \|\delta p\|^2 + \|\delta c_v\|^2\bigr),

where \mathcal{D}(t) is a non‑negative dissipation term. The coefficient C(t) is integrable on [0,T] because of the a priori bounds on the solutions. Grönwall’s inequality then implies \delta n \equiv \delta p \equiv \delta c_v \equiv 0 and consequently \delta\phi \equiv 0. Continuous dependence on data follows from the same argument. ∎

