Appendix E: Derivation of the Ion‑Migration V_{\text{oc}} Drift (Corollary 2.3.1)

At steady state, the Nernst–Planck equation with blocking boundary conditions integrates to zero flux, giving the Boltzmann distribution for the mobile vacancy concentration:

c_v^{\text{ss}}(z) = c_{v0}\,\frac{e^{q\phi(z)/k_BT_c}}{\langle e^{q\phi/k_BT_c}\rangle},\qquad
\langle e^{q\phi/k_BT_c}\rangle = \frac{1}{L_p}\int_0^{L_p} e^{q\phi(z)/k_BT_c}\,dz.\tag{E.1}

The ion accumulation modifies the electrostatic potential. Let \phi_0(z) be the solution of the Poisson equation without mobile ions (i.e., c_v=0). The presence of ions induces a perturbation \delta\phi satisfying

-\epsilon_p\frac{d^2\delta\phi}{dz^2} = q\bigl(c_v^{\text{ss}}(z) - c_{v0}\bigr).\tag{E.2}

Near the cathode (z=0), the potential is large, so c_v^{\text{ss}} is exponentially peaked within a Debye screening length \lambda_D = \sqrt{\epsilon_p k_BT_c/(q^2 c_{v0})}. Integrating (E.2) across the accumulation layer yields

-\epsilon_p\frac{d\delta\phi}{dz}\Big|_{z=0^+} \approx Q_{\text{ion}},\quad Q_{\text{ion}} = q\int_0^{L_p} (c_v^{\text{ss}} - c_{v0})\,dz,\tag{E.3}

where Q_{\text{ion}} is the areal density of accumulated ionic charge. The induced potential shift across the layer is \delta\phi(0) - \delta\phi(\infty) \approx -\frac{Q_{\text{ion}}\lambda_D}{\epsilon_p}. This directly reduces the built‑in potential and hence the open‑circuit voltage. To linear order,

\Delta V_{\text{oc}} \approx \delta\phi(L_p) - \delta\phi(0) \approx -\,\frac{Q_{\text{ion}}\lambda_D}{\epsilon_p}.\tag{E.4}

The right‑hand side has units of volts (C m⁻² × m / (F m⁻¹) = C m⁻¹ / (C V⁻¹ m⁻¹) = V), confirming dimensional consistency. For a MAPbI₃ cell with \epsilon_r=25, c_{v0}=10^{23}\,\text{m}^{-3}, and typical Q_{\text{ion}}\approx 10^{-4}\,\text{C m}^{-2}, one obtains \lambda_D\approx 2\,\text{nm} and \Delta V_{\text{oc}}\approx -9\,\text{mV}.

