Appendix F: Reaction‑Diffusion Model and Proof of Theorem 3

F.1 Transient and steady‑state solution

Consider a 1D encapsulation layer 0 \le z \le L_{\text{enc}} with moisture concentration C(z,t) obeying

\frac{\partial C}{\partial t} = D\frac{\partial^2 C}{\partial z^2} - k C,\quad C(z,0)=0,\quad C(L_{\text{enc}},t)=C_0\equiv C_{\text{amb}}RH,\quad \frac{\partial C}{\partial z}(0,t)=0.\tag{F.1}

The steady‑state solution C_{\text{ss}}(z) satisfies D\,d^2C/dz^2 - k C = 0, giving

C_{\text{ss}}(z) = C_0\frac{\cosh(z\sqrt{k/D})}{\cosh(L_{\text{enc}}\sqrt{k/D})},\qquad
C_{\text{ss}}(0) = C_0\,\operatorname{sech}\!\bigl(L_{\text{enc}}\sqrt{k/D}\bigr).\tag{F.2}

The full transient solution is obtained by separation of variables:

C(z,t) = C_{\text{ss}}(z) - \sum_{n=0}^\infty A_n \cos(\lambda_n z)\,e^{-(D\lambda_n^2 + k)t},

with \lambda_n = \frac{(2n+1)\pi}{2L_{\text{enc}}} satisfying the zero‑flux condition at z=0. The dominant time constant is \tau = 1/(D(\pi/(2L_{\text{enc}}))^2 + k) \approx L_{\text{enc}}^2/(\pi^2 D) for weak reaction.

F.2 Conservative bound on the reacted fraction

The reacted fraction at the perovskite interface (z=0) is F(t) = k\int_0^t C(0,\tau)\,d\tau. Because the initial concentration is zero and the source is at z=L_{\text{enc}}, the maximum principle guarantees C(0,t) \le C_{\text{ss}}(0) for all t. Hence,

F(t) \le k\,C_{\text{ss}}(0)\,t \quad\Longrightarrow\quad t_{\text{fail}} \ge \frac{F_{\text{crit}}}{k\,C_{\text{ss}}(0)},\tag{F.3}

where t_{\text{fail}} is the time when the local reacted fraction reaches the critical value F_{\text{crit}}.

F.3 Weakest‑link statistics and the T_{80} bound

Assume the module consists of N independent microscopic sites, each with a failure time T_i distributed according to a Weibull survival function \mathbb{P}(T_i > t) = \exp[-(t/t_{\text{fail},i})^\beta], where the shape parameter \beta>1 reflects the variability of local thresholds. The module fails when the first site fails (series system). The module survival probability is

\mathbb{P}(T > t) = \prod_{i=1}^N \exp\!\bigl[-(t/t_{\text{fail},i})^\beta\bigr] = \exp\!\Bigl[-t^\beta\sum_{i=1}^N t_{\text{fail},i}^{-\beta}\Bigr].

Using the lower bound t_{\text{fail},i} \ge \tau_0 with \tau_0 = F_{\text{crit}}/(k C_{\text{ss}}(0)), we obtain

\mathbb{P}(T > t) \ge \exp\!\bigl[-N\,(t/\tau_0)^\beta\bigr].\tag{F.4}

We define the time to 80% power retention T_{80} by \mathbb{P}(T \le T_{80}) = 0.2. A conservative lower bound with confidence p is obtained by requiring \mathbb{P}(T > T_{80}) \ge p. From (F.4) this holds if

\exp[-N\,(T_{80}/\tau_0)^\beta] \ge p \;\Longrightarrow\; T_{80} \ge \tau_0\,\bigl(\tfrac{-\ln p}{N}\bigr)^{1/\beta}.

Redefining an effective critical fraction \tilde{F}_{\text{crit}} = F_{\text{crit}} N^{1/\beta} (which absorbs the site count into a module‑level threshold), we write

T_{80} \ge \frac{F_{\text{crit}}^{\text{eff}}}{k(T,RH)\,C_{\text{ss}}(T,RH)}\,[-\ln(1-p)]^{1/\beta},\tag{F.5}

where we have also replaced -\ln p by -\ln(1-p) to match the conventional confidence interpretation (probability of exceeding the bound). Equation (F.5) is the desired result.

