Appendix G: Geometric Programming Formulation of LCOE Minimisation (Proof of Theorem 4)

G.1 Posynomial surrogate models

Near the optimal design point, the efficiency, lifetime, and cost can be approximated by posynomials—functions of the form \sum_k c_k \prod_j x_j^{a_{kj}} with c_k>0 and a_{kj}\in\mathbb{R}.

· Efficiency: From the analytic current‑matching condition and the saturation behaviour of absorption, the efficiency scales as \eta \propto d_1^{a_1} d_2^{a_2} \cdots with exponents close to zero. A local posynomial fit captures this behaviour.
· Lifetime: Theorem 3 gives \log T_{80} = \frac{E_a}{k_BT} - \ln D - \gamma\ln(\text{WVTR}) + \text{const}. Hence T_{80} itself is a monomial in the variables e^{1/T}, D^{-1}, and \text{WVTR}^{-\gamma}. After expressing D and WVTR in terms of encapsulation thickness and material properties, this becomes a posynomial.
· Cost: Manufacturing cost decomposes into material costs (proportional to layer volumes) and fixed processing steps, yielding a posynomial in thicknesses and areas.

G.2 LCOE as a posynomial

The Levelised Cost of Electricity is

\text{LCOE} = \frac{\text{CAPEX}\cdot\text{CRF} + \text{OPEX}}{P_{\text{rated}}\cdot\text{PR}\cdot\text{CF}\cdot 8760\cdot\eta_{\text{deg}}(T_{80})}.

Substituting the posynomial surrogates for CAPEX (thicknesses, areas), OPEX, and T_{80}, and noting that efficiency is a posynomial, the LCOE itself becomes a posynomial. Posynomials are closed under multiplication, division, and positive scaling, so the ratio inherits the posynomial structure.

G.3 Convex optimisation via logarithmic transformation

A geometric program is an optimisation problem of the form

\min_{\mathbf{x}>0}\; f_0(\mathbf{x}) \quad\text{subject to}\quad f_i(\mathbf{x})\le 1,\; i=1,\dots,m,\quad g_j(\mathbf{x})=1,\; j=1,\dots,p,

where f_i are posynomials and g_j are monomials. Under the change of variables \tilde{x}_j = \ln x_j, a monomial c\prod_j x_j^{a_j} becomes e^{a_0 + \sum_j a_j \tilde{x}_j}, and a posynomial becomes a sum of such exponentials. Taking the logarithm of a posynomial yields a log‑sum‑exp function, which is convex. The equality constraints g_j(\mathbf{x})=1 become linear equalities \sum_j a_{jk}\tilde{x}_j = -\ln c_k. The inequality constraints f_i(\mathbf{x})\le 1 become \ln f_i(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}) \le 0, which are convex. The objective \ln f_0(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}) is therefore convex in \tilde{\mathbf{x}}. Consequently, the transformed problem is a convex optimisation problem. Its stationary points are global minima; interior‑point methods find the unique global optimum in polynomial time. This establishes Theorem 4.

